    The privilege that reads a provider's discovery document, so that a provider URL can
    be checked while it is being typed. It makes this firewall fetch an address somebody
    chose, which is why it is a privilege of its own rather than something the login
    endpoints share.

    The endpoints under api/openidconnect/auth/ are deliberately NOT listed. They answer before
    anyone is signed in and decline the session check themselves; what protects them is
    the state and the nonce this firewall issued, not a privilege.

    Without this file the endpoint below is reachable by administrators alone, because
    core denies a page no ACL entry matches. That works, but it leaves the rule implicit
    and nowhere to be seen under System > Access > Groups.
-->
<acl>
    <page-system-openidconnect-discovery>
        <name>System: Access: Servers: OpenID Connect discovery</name>
        <patterns>
            <pattern>api/openidconnect/discovery/*</pattern>
        </patterns>
    </page-system-openidconnect-discovery>
</acl>
